-# Iteration 002 — DeepSeek-v4-pro: RQ2 Hint-Free Dispatch Harness Design
+### RQ4 - Scenario and Fixture Authoring, With Anti-Circularity
 
-**Model:** deepseek-v4-pro | **Iteration:** 2 of 5 | **Date:** 2026-05-30
+Consensus:
 
----
+Do not generate the main benchmark prompts directly from `INTENT_SIGNALS` keywords or trigger phrases. That tests keyword echo, not real discovery [minimax/iter3] [opus/iter2]. Generated variations are useful only when they start from domain-level task descriptions and pass contamination linting.
 
-## Focus
+Recommended fixture sources:
 
-Design a credible HINT-FREE dispatch harness for Lane C: run a realistic scenario against an AI and capture which references/assets it loaded plus its tool trace, WITHOUT leaking the expected answer/path into the prompt. This iteration answers three interconnected design questions: (a) how to instrument resource-load capture, (b) how to hold out the expected-resource key, and (c) how to avoid prompt contamination.
+| Source | Use | Circularity risk | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Production traffic | Primary source for external routing and real usage | Low | Use skill-graph/advisor traces where available [minimax/iter3] |
+| Hand-authored canonical | Coverage for declared skill duties | Low | Author from "what user needs", not router labels |
+| Hand-authored edge cases | Probe boundaries and failure modes | Low | Include should-not-activate and ambiguity cases |
+| Neighbor-skill-derived | Cross-skill disambiguation | Low/medium | Good for advisor confusion matrices |
+| Trigger/intent-derived smoke tests | Sanity check only | High | Score separately, never mix into main score |
+| Generated paraphrases | Scale coverage | Medium | Seed from domain briefs; linter blocks leaked terms |
 
----
+Minimum corpus:
 
-## Actions Taken
+MiniMax recommended 1-2 smoke tests per trigger phrase, 2-3 scenarios per intent key, 1-2 edge cases per known failure mode, 1-2 cross-skill disambiguation prompts, plus generated paraphrases from clean task-domain seeds. Small skills can start around 10-15 scenarios; complex skills with 10+ intent keys need 30-50 scenarios [minimax/iter3].
 
-1. **Re-read** deep-agent-improvement SKILL.md §2 (Smart Router) lines 97-214 — the `INTENT_SIGNALS` keyword-to-intent map, `RESOURCE_MAP` intent-to-path map, `RUNTIME_ASSETS` always/conditional loading, and `discover_markdown_resources()` as the canonical resource inventory function. These ARE the expected-resource key. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/deep-agent-improvement/SKILL.md:97-214]
+Anti-circularity controls:
 
-2. **Re-read** deep-agent-improvement SKILL.md §3-4 (Lane A/B structure) — the three pluggable seams (candidate-source, dispatcher, scorer) and the `--mode=` routing. The existing Lane B dispatcher (`dispatch-model.cjs`) already wraps external CLI invocations with structured return payloads (`{ok, exit_code, stdout, stderr, attempts}`). Lane C can extend this pattern with a `toolTrace` field. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/deep-agent-improvement/SKILL.md:273-282]
+- Split public prompt and private expected key [deepseek/iter2] [gpt55/iter2] [opus/iter2].
+- Use two-author or two-process separation: derive the expected key mechanically from `RESOURCE_MAP`; author the prompt from a paraphrased task brief [opus/iter2].
+- Run the same contamination logic as the router/advisor substring matcher [opus/iter2].
